# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Tosh Frontend — PRODUCTION (MAINNET) Environment Template
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Copy this file to `.env.production` (or wire into your hosting provider's
# secret store) and fill in every value before building.
#
# Anything prefixed `NEXT_PUBLIC_` is shipped to the browser.  Anything else
# stays server-side only.  Do not invert these by accident — the PoG signer
# key in particular MUST stay non-public.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ─── On-chain coordinates (mainnet — from DeployMainnet.s.sol output) ────────
NEXT_PUBLIC_FACTORY_ADDRESS=0xREPLACE_ME_FACTORY_ADDRESS
# This is the name `Deploy.s.sol` and `DeployMainnet.s.sol` print, so it is the
# one to copy. `NEXT_PUBLIC_LADDER_TREASURY` is an older alias that
# `contracts.ts` still accepts; do not set both.
NEXT_PUBLIC_TREASURY_ADDRESS=0xREPLACE_ME_LADDER_TREASURY
# Robinhood Chain. See docs/ROBINHOOD_MIGRATION.md, which supersedes the PM-B1
# "Ethereum L1" decision in docs/PRE_MAINNET_CHECKLIST.md §2.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CHAIN_ID=4663

# Uniswap V4 on Robinhood Chain — Uniswap's own deployment, verified on chain by
# code size at each address. Identical on testnet 46630, so these two lines are
# the same in a staging copy of this file.
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSITION_MANAGER=0x58daec3116aae6D93017bAAea7749052E8a04fA7
NEXT_PUBLIC_STATE_VIEW=0xF3334192D15450CdD385c8B70e03f9A6bD9E673b
# Permit2 is canonical across chains; override only if you know you need to.
NEXT_PUBLIC_PERMIT2=0x000000000022D473030F116dDEE9F6B43aC78BA3

# POOL_MANAGER is deliberately NOT here. It is hard-coded in
# src/lib/contracts.ts because a wrong value silently mis-CREATE2s every hook,
# so the cutover is a reviewed source change:
#
#   Robinhood Chain (4663 and 46630)  0x8366a39CC670B4001A1121B8F6A443A643e40951
#
# Note what changed with the move off Base: mainnet and testnet share this
# address, so the edit no longer takes staging down when it lands. The salts and
# the hook implementation's address still derive from it, so it still belongs
# with a deploy — but the "one or the other" tension that made it PM-B2's
# problem is gone.

# ─── RPC fallback list ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Premium endpoint goes first; the wagmi config (`providers.tsx`) falls back to
# the public endpoint automatically if this one flaps. The server uses the same
# value (`app/lib/serverRpc.ts`).
#
# Use NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL, not a chain-named variable. This line used to read
#
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_SEPOLIA_RPC=https://eth-mainnet.g.alchemy.com/v2/...
#
# — a variable naming one chain, set to an endpoint for another, in the file an
# operator follows during the mainnet cutover. Chain-named variables are now
# honoured only on the chain they name, so that value would simply be ignored;
# NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL names no chain and always applies to NEXT_PUBLIC_CHAIN_ID.
#
# Must point at the SAME chain as NEXT_PUBLIC_CHAIN_ID above. The routes that
# authenticate against on-chain state verify this at runtime and fail closed if
# the endpoint reports a different id.
#
# Leave it blank to use Robinhood's public endpoint. That endpoint is rate
# limited and carries no SLA, which is survivable for wallet traffic and is not
# survivable for an indexer — so production should name a dedicated provider.
NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL=https://rpc.mainnet.chain.robinhood.com

# Testnet only — ignored unless NEXT_PUBLIC_CHAIN_ID=46630. Left here for
# staging deploys that share this template.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ROBINHOOD_TESTNET_RPC=https://rpc.testnet.chain.robinhood.com

# ─── API hardening ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Comma-separated list of origins allowed to call the /api/* routes.
# In production, this should contain EXACTLY your domain(s).  A missing /
# blank value falls CLOSED — no origin is allowed.  Do NOT use `*`.
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://tosh.example,https://app.tosh.example

# ─── Shared rate-limit store (PM-F5) ────────────────────────────────────────
# Both blank → the limiter counts in process memory.  That is correct locally
# and in CI, and WRONG in production: every instance gets its own full quota,
# and a rolling deploy resets all of them.  On a platform that scales per
# request that is close to having no rate limit at all — and nothing errors,
# so the limiter reports healthy while enforcing almost nothing.
#
# Upstash REST rather than a Redis TCP client on purpose: no dependency to
# audit, and it works in the edge runtime where a socket client does not.
# Any REST-compatible endpoint speaking the same pipeline API will do.
#
# The token is a real credential (read/write to the counter namespace) — keep
# it in the platform secret store, never NEXT_PUBLIC_.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=

# The same two variables also back the live Proof-of-Gas exchange rate, which
# has the same per-instance failure mode for a worse reason: without a shared
# store an owner-signed rate rotation lands on whichever instance served the
# POST, and every other instance keeps signing allocations at the old rate.

# ─── Proof-of-Gas · the indexer key, and why blank is not a soft failure ─────
# Free key from dev.blockscout.com — no card, and one key covers every chain.
#
# Blank does not degrade the scan, it throttles the product. Measured against
# the unkeyed public instances on 2026-09-05, by walking each host to its limit:
#
#   Ethereum, Optimism   x-ratelimit-limit: 180 per ~60 s   → 3 req/s, fine
#   Arbitrum, Base       x-ratelimit-limit:  10 per ~40 min → 429 on the tenth
#
# A scan reads all five chains and EVERY one must succeed, because an unreachable
# chain reads as a smaller wallet and that difference is an allocation. So the
# two tightest hosts cap the whole launch at roughly TEN WALLETS AN HOUR, and the
# eleventh claimant of each hour gets a 503. `/api/pog-scan` sizes its own global
# ceiling off this variable for exactly that reason: 10/hour blank, 40/hour set.
#
# Free tier is 5 req/s and 100k credits/day at ~20 credits a call — about 5,000
# calls/day, and a light scan is five calls, so roughly a thousand wallets a day.
# $49/mo raises it to 15 req/s and 100M credits/month. Paid tiers buy throughput,
# NOT coverage: Etherscan does not index Robinhood Chain at any price, which is
# why this is Blockscout in the first place.
#
# A real credential. Platform secret store, never NEXT_PUBLIC_.
BLOCKSCOUT_API_KEY=

# ─── Rate limiting · how many proxies sit in front of this app ───────────────
# `X-Forwarded-For` is a list each hop APPENDS to, so the rightmost entry is
# what the nearest proxy observed and the leftmost is whatever the CLIENT
# claimed.  Trusting the leftmost lets a caller mint itself a fresh rate-limit
# bucket per request with one header — including on /api/sign-allocation.
#
# Set this to the number of trusted proxies between the internet and this app:
#   1  — Vercel, Cloudflare, or a single nginx.  The default, and almost always
#        right.  Platform headers (`cf-connecting-ip`, `x-vercel-forwarded-for`)
#        are preferred over XFF when present, so this rarely has to change.
#   0  — nothing trustworthy in front; XFF and X-Real-IP are ignored entirely.
#   N  — N chained proxies you control.
RATE_LIMIT_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1

# ─── Supabase ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The anon key is safe to expose and is meant to be: it is the key the browser
# holds, and row-level security is what makes that safe rather than the key
# being secret.  Run supabase/migrations/0001_projects_rls.sql before pointing
# production at a project — under those policies `anon` may SELECT and nothing
# more.  Verify with `npm run check:supabase`, which asks the live project
# rather than the source: it attempts INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE as anon and
# fails if any of them is allowed.
#
# New projects issue `sb_publishable_…` / `sb_secret_…` keys rather than the
# legacy anon/service_role JWTs.  Either works, and the roles behind them are
# the same ones the migration's policies name; the new pair does not expire and
# can be revoked individually.  The variable names below do not change.
#
# The URL is the BARE project URL.  Do not paste the `/rest/v1/` form the
# dashboard shows next to the keys — the SDK appends that itself.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://REPLACE_ME.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=REPLACE_ME_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY

# The writer, and a real credential: it BYPASSES row-level security entirely.
# Anyone holding it can rewrite any project's name, logo and outbound links.
# Server-side secret store only, never NEXT_PUBLIC_, and not in this file with
# a value in it.
#
# Needed because the checks that authorise a write — read the launch from
# chain, recover the signer, require it to equal the creator — live in
# POST /api/projects and cannot be expressed as a row predicate.  Leave it
# unset and every read still works; publishing a new project is what stops,
# with a 503 that names this variable.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ─── PoG signing service (SERVER-SIDE ONLY) ─────────────────────────────────
# • A NEW key at mainnet cutover, distinct from the deployer, written here in
#   Vercel Production only. Never NEXT_PUBLIC_*. See PRE_MAINNET_CHECKLIST §4.1.
# • There is no KMS path. `sign-allocation/route.ts` reads this variable.
#   A second name, POG_PRIVATE_KEY, is an alias and must equal this one if set.
POG_SIGNER_PRIVATE_KEY=
POG_PRIVATE_KEY=

# ─── Admin-config shared secret (optional fallback) ─────────────────────────
# Empty by default — the on-chain-owner-signature path is preferred.  Set this
# only if you genuinely need a CI / emergency-rollback bearer token.
ADMIN_SECRET=

# ─── Error monitoring — Sentry (pre-mainnet item #26) ───────────────────────
# Leave the DSNs blank and the SDK installs nothing: no requests, no events,
# no cost.  That is the correct setting for staging; mainnet must fill them.
#
# A DSN is a write-only ingest key, not a credential — NEXT_PUBLIC_ is safe.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=
# Only set this if server events should land in a different Sentry project
# than browser events.  Blank → the server reuses the public DSN above.
SENTRY_DSN=
# Tags every event so a staging alert never pages the mainnet on-call.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=production
#
# Source-map upload at build time.  All three must be present or the build
# skips the upload entirely (it never fails the build over a missing token).
# The auth token is a real credential — keep it in the CI secret store only.
SENTRY_ORG=
SENTRY_PROJECT=
SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=
